  undesired:
    - Any hardcoded list of doc names anywhere in the service. A markdown file added under a content root appears as a new route on the next build with no other edit.
    - "Silent failures: a file that exists on disk but is missing from the registry, or a commit hash that defaults to empty instead of the named uncommitted marker."
    - Non-deterministic ordering, where a route's sort position changes between builds (for example by modification time or disk order), breaking stable addresses and making the registry impossible to diff.
    - A route whose address is not lowercase or does not sit under the docs area in its own section.
    - A rendered link to an existing doc that still points at a .md file path, a relative path, or a route that does not exist, such as a link whose case differs from the lowercase route it targets. A link to a missing doc that passes the build with no warning, or is rewritten into a guessed route.
    - A build or deploy date standing in for a doc's published or last-modified date, or filling a date that cannot be known; a missing date stays missing. A markdown copy that still carries frontmatter or relative links that break off the site.
---

## Context

The docs area is the markdown that ships with the package: methodology docs, commands, agents, skills, and the tool and hook references, six content roots each mapped to one docs section. The registry turns each file into a route at build time. Discovery, not enumeration, is what makes "add a file, get a route" a structural guarantee rather than a habit.

Each route is read twice. Doc pages render it; the site page list reads its dates, and from there the sitemap, feed, llms files and structured data take them. Only the markdown copies and llms-full need a doc's body, and they ask for it. A wrong fact here therefore surfaces on every machine-readable surface at once.

Three facts per route come from version history: the commit that last touched the file, which pins the cite-as link; the first-published date; and the last-modified date. Only full shared history knows them. A shallow checkout answers every date query with the one commit it holds, so every doc looks new and the answers look real.

The markdown body is read off the site, by coding agents and llms-full readers, where frontmatter is noise and a relative link points nowhere. The rendered HTML is read on the site, where a link must land on a live route and a link to a section must land on its heading.

## Strategy

**Discover every markdown file under the six roots, filtered by extension only, once per build.** No allowlist, denylist or name pattern exists, so a new file is a new route on the next build with nothing else to edit. The walk runs once and every page in the build reads that one result, because a second walk could disagree with the first and two pages would then describe different doc sets.

**Keep each doc's name in its file's case; lowercase only the address.** Labels and titles read the name its author gave (Stop, aide/SKILL); the address, static params and every link to it are lowercase, so readers and citations meet one spelling. Lookup by section and name ignores case. Two files differing only by case would claim one address, so the build fails naming both, because dropping either is a silent missing route.

**Order routes by section in the site's category order, then each section's index first, then by lowercased address.** Every tier is a property of the file's path, never its modification time or disk order, so the order is identical on every build and the registry diffs cleanly. Index-first makes each section's landing page its first route; sorting by the lowercased address orders mixed-case names the way readers see them.

**Take every version fact from the full shared history, through the one date resolver every surface uses.** Each route carries its last commit, first-published date and last-modified date. A shallow history fails the build, because it yields wrong dates that look right. An untracked file carries the named uncommitted marker and no dates: a missing date stays missing, never filled by build or deploy time, so the page, sitemap, feed and structured data agree.

**Give each doc's markdown body, frontmatter stripped and links absolute, only on request, once per doc per build.** Its links pass through the same resolution as the rendered HTML, so a doc's two outputs never point at different pages. The site-files script asks on behalf of the AI-facing outputs, so no surface parses markdown itself. Building the registry never produces a body: the link rewrite dominated its cost, and doc pages, sidebar and sitemap never read it.

**Resolve in-doc links against the whole route set by file path at render time.** Each relative link resolves against the linking doc's location and is matched to a route by file, never by rewriting the href, so cross-root and index links land on live routes; anchors are kept, external links untouched. A missing target warns naming link and doc, and stays as written. One code highlighter serves every render in the build.

**Give every rendered heading an id derived from its text, numbered in order when repeated.** The docs are written and read on GitHub too, so the id follows GitHub's heading-anchor rule: a section link written there lands on the same heading here. Derived from text, not position, the id survives edits elsewhere in the doc, so a cited section link keeps working across releases.

## Good examples

The Stop hook page: discovered from the hooks root with no list naming it, labelled `Stop`, served at `/docs/hooks/stop/`, carrying its last commit, the date it first entered shared history and the date of the last commit touching it (illustrative: `b75834c`, 2026-11-26, 2026-11-27). The sitemap, the page's visible date and its structured data all show that same last-modified date.

The hooks index, rendered and copied. In its HTML, `[Stop](./Stop.md)` becomes `href="/docs/hooks/stop/"` and the cross-root `[aide_observer_event](../tools/aide_observer_event.md)` becomes `href="/docs/tools/aide_observer_event/"`. Its markdown copy opens on the first heading, with no frontmatter block, and reads `[Stop](https://aidemd.dev/docs/hooks/stop/)`, so an agent holding only the copy can follow it.

The heading `### Brevity Contract` renders with id `brevity-contract`, so the link `./aide-spec.md#brevity-contract` in the template doc, written for GitHub, lands on it at `/docs/methodology/aide-spec/#brevity-contract`. A new untracked doc shows commit `uncommitted` and no dates.

## Bad examples

A deploy that checks out one commit of history builds cleanly, and every doc reports that commit's day as both first-published and last-modified. The sitemap tells search engines every doc changed today, the feed lists every doc as new, and nothing fails, because the dates look real.

A link rewritten by string-munging, `./Stop.md` to `/docs/hooks/Stop` or `../tools/x.md` to `/docs/hooks/tools/x`, renders and passes a markdown check but 404s on the site. Equally wrong: a link to a missing doc passing with no warning, or rewritten into a guessed route.

A markdown copy that opens with the doc's frontmatter block and keeps `./Stop.md`: on the site it looks fine, but an agent reading the copy from llms-full or the `.md` URL gets metadata as prose and a link that resolves to nothing.

## References

- `.aide/intent.aide` -- root outcome "every page tells the truth about itself to machines" (the shown last-updated date is true and matches the sitemap and structured data) and the undesired "a build-time date anywhere a true last-updated date belongs"; root Strategy decisions on the zero-exclusion filesystem walk, the commit-pinned cite-as link, and one true date per page feeding every machine-readable surface (a missing date omitted, never build-stamped)
- `src/app/_shared/listSitePages/.aide` -- the downstream reader of each route's dates (it never reads bodies; `scripts/publishSiteFiles/.aide` asks for them, for the markdown copies and llms-full); its undesired outcome that a shallow-history build fails rather than ship plausible wrong dates
- Direct owner instructions (in-doc links, confirmed 2026-11-26) -- links resolved by file path through the registry, never string-munged; a missing target warns naming link and doc and is left as written, the build passes
- Direct owner instructions (lowercase URLs, confirmed 2026-11-26) -- only the address is lowercased; names keep the file's case for labels; index first, then lowercased address; case-insensitive lookup
- Direct owner instructions (full SEO/AEO scope, confirmed 2026-11-26) -- true dateModified, a markdown copy of each docs page, llms-full.txt
- Direct owner instructions (SEO/AEO surface tree, confirmed 2026-11-27) -- full history in the deploy, one shared date resolver that fails on a shallow repo, routes carrying the true last-modified date and a markdown body with frontmatter stripped and links absolute, heading ids
- Direct owner instructions ("Rewrite on demand", decided 2026-11-27) -- routes no longer carry the body; it is given on request for the AI-facing outputs, because the link rewrite was ~1.8s of the ~2s registry build
- `src/data/docsCategories.ts` -- category order that sets section priority; each root's section and URL segment
- `src/types/docs.ts` -- the route, registry and route-key shapes the registry satisfies
